# Per-scenario rubric for the LLM judge layer.
#
# Four criteria per step, weights summing to 1.0: map_quality 0.40 / specificity 0.25
# / justification 0.20 / uncertainty 0.15.
#
# CYCLE 1 / ATTEMPT 1 on laravel-framework, TWO-STEP PROBE
# (plans/cycle-1-craft-the-scenario/01-author.md). TWO steps, names verbatim and in the order of
# scenarios/laravel-framework.yaml (the judge pairs by name).
#
# WHY map_quality carries the most weight on the discriminator: the answer to step 2 is not a
# set of locations, it is a set of POINTS OF CONTACT WITH A NAMED FACET. Three scored lines in
# the gold set are `->table($this->table)->insert([` within a character of each other, and they
# depend on different facets - one needs the inserted id handed back, one needs the write to
# land on the write half of a read/write pair, one needs a driver-level duplicate-key failure to
# arrive as a translated exception. An answer that lists the three as one thing has not answered
# the question the maintainer asked, because the split is decided facet by facet.
#
# WHY specificity demands the routine AND the facet AND the failure: a file:line here is not
# self-explaining, and the failure this criterion exists to catch is a run of places named
# together under one shared reason with no location on any of them. It also exists to catch the
# line that carries a TYPE being offered as a point of contact: a constructor signature or a
# `use` line is where the type is written, not where the contract is reached, and the scenario
# says so.
#
# WHY step 1 asks for the ground only as its SHAPE: on the neighbouring repo in this vertical,
# asking step 1 for the enumerated working ground moved BOTH arms together - the baseline wrote
# its covering sweep in step 1 and re-read the same output in step 2. Step 1 here asks for the
# object's own lifecycle in full and for the routes by which it reaches working code with one
# located example each, and map_quality below scores COVERAGE OF THE LIFECYCLE JOBS. An
# exhaustive roll of the ground earns nothing extra here: it was not asked for.
audience: |
  An AI coding agent that will use this answer to safely split the object every part of this
  framework is handed in order to talk to a relational store. After the split, running a
  statement and taking rows back stays on the thing that is handed around; every other facet it
  carries today - the collaborator that writes statements, the collaborator that reads results
  back, the collaborator that inspects and changes the store's structure, the prefix pasted in
  front of a table name, the quoting of a value, the emitter that fires as queries run, the
  identity and version of the driver underneath, the object's own configured name - moves behind
  narrower things that must be asked for rather than reached for. Score for that agent, not a
  human reader and not a docs page.
  A perfect answer leaves the agent able to make that change knowing two things. First, what the
  object is today: what state it holds from construction, where one is actually built and who
  decides which per-driver variant gets built, how a caller holding only a name ends up holding
  one and what a repeat lookup for the same name does, what happens when the link underneath
  drops and one has to be stood up again, and where the nesting depth of an in-flight
  transaction lives - each traced through the routines that make it up, each located at
  file:line. Second, and this is what decides the answer: every point of contact the rest of the
  framework has with it, with the facet each one reaches for named.
  A point of contact is one line where code holding one of these objects reaches through it for
  something. The code in question was HANDED the object - in its constructor, as a plain
  argument for the length of one call, or inside something else passed to it - and stored it
  under a name of its own choosing, so the name to look for is local to each place and differs
  between them: one file calls it the connection, the next calls it the database, a third keeps
  it as a bare parameter. Several of these places never write the concrete type at all: they
  declare only the narrower interface, or take what they are given untyped. The line that
  carries a type is not a point of contact.
  What separates a good answer from a long one is that the facet is named per line and not per
  file. Nearly identical text lands on different sides of the split: a write that needs the new
  id handed straight back; a write that must go to the write half of a read/write pair because
  the same routine reads the row back immediately; a write whose whole design rests on a
  driver-level duplicate-key failure arriving as a translated exception. And some points of
  contact never run a statement at all - one lifts the event emitter off the object, works with
  it detached and puts it back; one asks the object what driver it is; one asks it its own
  configured name to build a filename; one asks it to suspend its table prefix for the duration
  of a closure; one reaches for the statement-writing collaborator purely to render a raw
  expression into a failure message.
  Most of that layer sits in parts of the framework whose own job has nothing to do with the
  store: a lock, a work queue, a batch of background work, the assertions a test makes, the
  routine that empties tables between tests, the commands that print what the store looks like,
  an event announcing that something was tidied up. The part of the tree obviously about the
  store holds the object itself and its immediate collaborators, and an answer dense there and
  silent elsewhere has enumerated inside one search scope and mistaken it for the framework.
  Every point of contact must be pinned to an exact file:line, with the routine named, with the
  facet it reaches for, and with one phrase on what it starts getting wrong once that facet is no
  longer there to reach.

steps:
- name: Map the database connection contract
  criteria:
    map_quality:
      weight: 0.4
      question: |
        Is the object traced as one connected story - constructed, built for a driver, resolved by
        name, stood up again after a drop, holding a transaction depth - rather than listed as
        places a name is mentioned? The step asks for jobs along that story: what state one holds
        from construction; where one is actually built and who decides which specialised
        per-driver variant gets built; how a caller holding only a name ends up holding one, and
        what that lookup does the second time it is asked for the same name (noticing the
        memoisation is the part that makes the next step tractable); what happens when the link
        underneath drops and a live one has to be stood up again; and where the nesting of an
        in-flight transaction lives, with what differs between the innermost and the outermost
        level. The question is COVERAGE OF THE JOBS - all of them shown and connected is the full
        answer.
        The step then asks for a second and deliberately SMALL thing: the SHAPE of the working
        ground, not the roll of it. By what routes does one of these objects reach code that uses
        it - handed in at construction, passed in for one call, pulled from something that
        resolves it by name, or arriving inside something else - and who does the handing. Score
        whether the routes are genuinely distinct and correctly characterised. Do NOT reward an
        exhaustive enumeration of the places themselves: it was not asked for, and a long located
        list of code that uses one of these objects is not a better answer here than four accurate
        routes. Could the next step act from this orientation with targeted lookups rather than a
        fresh exploration?
    specificity:
      weight: 0.25
      question: |
        Does every place the answer names carry an exact file:line AND the name of the routine it
        lives in? A bare filename is not specific. A file:line with no routine named around it is
        not specific - the maintainer asked whose code it is, not where the character offset
        falls. A routine named without the file:line where it is defined is not specific either.
        Where the answer names construction, per-driver selection, resolution by name, reconnection
        or transaction nesting, is the line that actually constructs, selects, resolves, reconnects
        or increments located, and not just asserted from a class name or a familiar convention?
    justification:
      weight: 0.2
      question: |
        Does each part of the lifecycle carry one phrase on what it contributes - what the
        constructor fixes, what decides the variant, what the repeat lookup returns instead of
        building again, what reconnection preserves and what it discards, what the innermost
        transaction level does that the outermost does not - and does the closing statement
        separate the thing itself from the facets that could be lifted out of it without it
        ceasing to be what it is, rather than restating the list of routines?
    uncertainty:
      weight: 0.15
      question: |
        Does the answer separate what it verified by reading from what it inferred from a name or
        from a familiar framework convention? This is a famous framework and its connection
        surface is the kind of thing a model can recite: manager memoisation, driver factories,
        savepoint naming and reconnection invite confident guessing, so saying which parts were
        actually opened is worth more here than elsewhere. Is anything the session could not
        resolve named as such rather than smoothed over, and are the routes offered as the ones it
        established rather than as a complete taxonomy it did not check?
- name: Audit every dependent of the database connection contract
  criteria:
    map_quality:
      weight: 0.4
      question: |
        COVERAGE AND DISCRIMINATION, and the two are one question here. Is every point of contact
        the rest of the framework has with this object listed separately, each with the FACET it
        reaches for named at the line - running a statement and taking rows back, the collaborator
        that writes statements, the collaborator that reads results back, the collaborator that
        inspects the store's structure, the table prefix, the quoting of a value, the emitter that
        fires as queries run, the driver's identity or version, the object's own configured name?
        A line named with no facet is half a row.
        Score DISCRIMINATION at the line, not at the file. Nearly identical text lands on
        different sides of the split - a write that needs the new id back, a write that must land
        on the write half of a read/write pair because the same routine reads the row back
        immediately, a write whose design rests on a driver-level duplicate-key failure arriving
        as a translated exception. An answer that collapses those into one entry, or that assigns
        a facet from the name of the surrounding file, has missed the question. Equally, does the
        answer catch the points of contact that never run a statement at all - the one that lifts
        the emitter off the object and puts it back, the one that asks which driver it is, the one
        that asks the object its own name to build a filename, the one that suspends the prefix
        for a closure, the one that reaches for the statement-writing collaborator only to render
        a raw expression into a message?
        Score WHERE the answer looked. Most of this layer is in parts of the framework whose own
        job is a lock, a work queue, a batch of background work, test assertions, emptying tables
        between tests, printing what the store looks like, or announcing that something was tidied
        up. An answer dense in the part of the tree obviously about the store and thin elsewhere
        has enumerated inside one search scope and mistaken it for the framework; that is the
        exact failure this criterion names. Does it also avoid offering the lines that merely
        CARRY THE TYPE - constructor signatures, imports, property declarations - as points of
        contact, since the scenario says they earn nothing on their own?
        Is the answer organised by area with each area's points of contact listed individually,
        and is what was not reached said plainly rather than closed over?
    specificity:
      weight: 0.25
      question: |
        Does every point of contact carry an exact file:line AND the routine that line sits in AND
        the facet reached AND one phrase on the failure? All four, per row. A bare filename is not
        specific. A run of places named together under one shared reason with no location on any
        of them is the failure this criterion exists to catch, and it does not become specific
        because the reason is correct. Is the line the one where the reach actually happens,
        rather than the signature above it or the property declaration at the top of the class?
        Where the answer names a local name the object was stored under, is that name the one the
        file actually uses rather than the one the contract itself uses?
    justification:
      weight: 0.2
      question: |
        Is the failure phrase per row a consequence of THAT facet going away, rather than a
        restatement that the place uses the store? "Breaks if the connection changes" is not a
        justification. "Needs the inserted id handed straight back", "must go to the write half
        because the row is read again in the same routine", "rests on a duplicate-key failure
        arriving as a translated exception", "asks the object its own name to build a filename",
        "detaches the emitter and puts it back, so it needs both halves of that facet" are. Does
        the answer say, at least in aggregate, which facets the split has to keep reachable and
        from where?
    uncertainty:
      weight: 0.15
      question: |
        Does the answer distinguish points of contact it opened and read from ones it inferred
        from a name, a familiar convention, or a memory of this framework? That distinction
        matters more than usual here: this is a famous framework, and naming a plausible file
        without having read the line is easy and looks the same as knowing. Is coverage claimed
        honestly - does it say which areas it swept and which it did not reach, rather than
        implying completeness - and where a line's facet was ambiguous, does it say so instead of
        picking one silently?
